@startuml
!include https://raw.githubusercontent.com/proveo-ca/identity/main/proveo.puml
skinparam noteTextAlignment left
' purpose: attaching an editor to a sandbox — why the workspace MODE decides what that even means, and the data-loss shape hiding in the default
' status: proposed 2026-09-10, not implemented; _spec/_plans/ is exempt from the SPEC-reference rule.
' would touch: internal/sbx/** (there is NO ssh support today — measured by grep)
' would touch: internal/backend/sandbox/sandbox.go (Publish, PreserveClone, CloneRescueLines)
' would touch: internal/run/run.go (decideClone), internal/workspace/mount.go
'   what a clone is and how work returns _spec/internal/sbx/clone-workspace.puml
'   the cwd hazard an editor would hit .. _spec/internal/sbx/virtiofs-cwd-invalidation.puml
'   the publish mechanism it needs ...... _spec/_plans/tools-and-localhost-tunnels.puml
'   where it would be offered ........... _spec/_plans/post-close-surface.puml
'   what else lives in the home mount ... _spec/_experiments/operator-fingerprint.puml
' Notes are capped at 5 lines x 50 columns; one idea per anchor.
'
' ── AUTHORITATIVE SOURCES ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
'   sbx ssh (community write-up) : https://www.ajeetraina.com/ssh-straight-into-your-agent-sandboxes-a-hands-on-look-at-sbx-ssh/
'
' ── WHERE THIS STARTS: proveo HAS NO ssh PATH AT ALL ───────────────────────
' Measured, not assumed: `internal/sbx` contains no ssh code. `sbx ssh` exists
' upstream and sbx-kit-capabilities.puml already cites it among its sources, but
' nothing in proveo invokes it, wraps it, or publishes a port for it. So this
' item starts from zero rather than from a partial implementation.
'
' ── THE MODE DECIDES WHAT "CONNECT" MEANS, AND THE TWO MEANINGS CONFLICT ───
' This is not one feature. It is two, with opposite semantics, and the workspace
' mode picks which one the operator gets:
'
'   DIRECT MODE   the workspace IS the host tree. An attached editor edits the
'                 operator's real files. That is what they expect — and it also
'                 dissolves the isolation the sandbox was providing for edits,
'                 because now two writers share one tree with no coordination.
'   CLONE MODE    the DEFAULT on sbx. The editor edits the CLONE. Edits never
'                 appear on the host, and they are LOST when the sandbox is
'                 removed unless fetched from `sandbox-<name>` or pushed.
'
' AN EDITOR THAT SILENTLY EDITS A DISPOSABLE COPY IS A DATA-LOSS SHAPE, and it
' is the one the default produces. Someone attaches an IDE, works for an hour,
' closes the sandbox, and their work is in refs nobody told them about. The
' first job of this plan is therefore not the transport — it is deciding which
' mode is supported and making the other LOUD.
'
' ── AND CLONE MODE ALREADY HAS THE RETURN PATH, IN PROSE ───────────────────
' `CloneRescueLines` is the by-hand recipe for getting work out of a clone, and
' clone-workspace.puml documents the two routes home. So the machinery exists;
' what is missing is that an IDE user never sees it, because it is printed at
' teardown to a terminal they were not looking at.
'
' Which links this item to the post-close surface: "fetch the clone's refs"
' being an ACTION rather than a recipe is what makes clone-mode IDE attachment
' survivable. The two plans are not independent.
'
' ── A SECOND WRITER IN ONE TREE IS ITS OWN PROBLEM ────────────────────────
' Whichever mode, attaching WHILE THE AGENT RUNS puts two writers in one working
' tree with no locking. The agent rewrites a file the operator has open; the
' editor writes back from a stale buffer; the agent's next read sees neither.
' Nothing in proveo mediates that, and nothing in an IDE expects to.
'
' virtiofs-cwd-invalidation.puml records a related hazard already measured on
' this stack — a workspace dentry vanishing under a live agent — so the
' filesystem underneath is known to be less stable than an editor assumes.
'
' THE CHEAPER FRAMING: attach to a KEPT sandbox after the agent has exited. One
' writer, no race, and the post-close surface is exactly where the offer belongs.
' A live attach is a bigger feature wearing the same name.
'
' ── ATTACHING AN IDE WIDENS WHO READS THE MOUNT ───────────────────────────
' An editor in the sandbox is another process with the agent's mounts and the
' agent's credentials. Measured from inside a live sandbox: `/proveo-home/logs/`
' carries EVERY prior run's transcript, readable and writable. So attaching an
' IDE hands a second party — the editor, its extensions, and whatever they
' phone home to — the same access, over a path nobody chose for that purpose.
'
' The mount narrowing named in operator-fingerprint.puml is therefore a
' PRECONDITION for this rather than an unrelated cleanup. Extensions are the
' sharp end: a language server or a telemetry-happy plugin reads the whole
' workspace by design.
'
' ── THE CANDIDATE TRANSPORTS, AND WHAT EACH DEMANDS ───────────────────────
'   sbx ssh                    upstream already has it; proveo would wrap it
'                              rather than build one. Least new surface.
'   VS Code Remote-SSH         needs an ssh endpoint, then installs a server
'                              into the sandbox — a write the image did not plan
'                              for, and a version it does not pin.
'   devcontainer attach        speaks to a container, not a microVM; how it maps
'                              onto sbx is unmeasured.
'   JetBrains Gateway          same ssh dependency, heavier in-sandbox footprint.
' All four need a reachable endpoint, which is the publish mechanism from
' tools-and-localhost-tunnels.puml. That plan should land first.
title PLANNED — connect an IDE: the workspace mode decides\nwhat "connect" means, and the default loses work

package "the operator's machine" as HOST <<host>> {
  component "the editor\nplus its extensions" as IDE <<app>>
  component "the host tree" as TREE <<app>>
}
package "the sandbox" as SB <<host>> {
  component "the clone\nDEFAULT on sbx" as CLONE <<error>>
  component "the agent\nstill writing, maybe" as AG <<app>>
  database """/proveo-home""\nevery prior run's transcript" as HOME <<error>>
}
component "**work in refs\nnobody mentioned**" as LOST <<error>>
component "no ssh path in proveo\nmeasured, not assumed" as NOSSH <<error>>

IDE ARROW_OPTIONAL NOSSH : the transport that\ndoes not exist yet
IDE ARROW_MAIN CLONE : direct mode edits TREE;\nclone mode edits THIS
CLONE ARROW_ERROR LOST : removed sandbox, unless\nfetched or pushed
IDE ARROW_ERROR HOME : an extension reads the mount\nit was never granted
AG ARROW_ERROR CLONE : a second writer, no locking
TREE ARROW_OPTIONAL IDE

note bottom of CLONE
  **The default is the dangerous one.** On sbx the
  editor edits a disposable copy. An hour's work
  lands in ""refs/proveo/<sid>/"" and nobody said
  so, because the recipe prints at
  teardown to a terminal they left.
end note

note bottom of HOME
  **An IDE widens who reads the mount.** Measured:
  ""/proveo-home/logs/"" holds every prior run's
  transcript, readable and writable. Extensions
  read a workspace by design, so the mount
  narrowing is a precondition, not a cleanup.
end note

note bottom of AG
  **Two writers, no mediation.** Attaching while
  the agent runs races on every file. Nothing in
  proveo arbitrates it and no IDE expects to —
  and the filesystem underneath already loses a
  cwd under a live agent.
end note

note right of NOSSH
  **This starts at zero.** ""internal/sbx"" has no
  ssh code; ""sbx ssh"" exists upstream and proveo
  does not call it. Wrapping it is less surface
  than building a transport.
end note

legend bottom
  **The order to do it in**
  ==
  **1. Decide which mode is supported, and make the other loud.** Clone mode is the
  default and it silently edits a copy; an IDE attached to it must say so, before any
  transport work. This is the whole risk of the item.
  --
  **2. Attach AFTER the agent exits, first.** One writer, no race, and the post-close
  surface is where the offer belongs. A live attach is a larger feature wearing the same
  name and should be argued separately.
  ==
  **3. Narrow the mount before inviting extensions in.** An editor brings third-party
  code with the agent's access, over a home mount that currently exposes every prior
  run's transcript.
  --
  **4. Wrap ""sbx ssh"" rather than building a transport**, and land the publish mechanism
  from tools-and-localhost-tunnels first — every candidate needs a reachable endpoint.
  --
  **Left open**: whether a Remote-SSH server writing itself into the image is acceptable
  given nothing pins its version, and how devcontainer attach maps onto a microVM at all.
end legend
@enduml
